# Symbulator circuit book

title: Circuits for Lesson 4 of the Tutorial (Part 1)

[HK5's Drill Problem 1-13]

r1,1,0,25
jd,0,2,0.2*v1
r2,2,3,10
ji,4,3,2.5
r3,4,5,100
s1,1,2
s2,2,4
s3,0,3
s4,3,5

analysis: dc
note: In this problem, we use short circuits (the s element) for the first time — four of them, one for each current the problem asks about. Find i1, i2, i3 and i4, on the current through line of each short's block.
image: https://learn.symbulator.com/assets/circuit/hk5d0113.jpg
rounding: approx
si: no
units: yes


[AS2's Practice Problem 2.15]

e,a,0,100
r13,a,1,13
r24,1,2,24
r10,1,3,10
r20,2,3,20
r30,2,0,30
r50,3,0,50

analysis: dc
note: Find the equivalent resistance as seen by the 100 V source, and the current i. The equivalent resistance comes free here: a source sits between the two nodes, so re is already an answer.
image: https://learn.symbulator.com/assets/circuit/as2pp0215.jpg [462px]
rounding: exact
si: no
units: yes


[B11's Example 8.29 (step by step)]

r4,0,a,4
r2,0,b,2
r6,a,b,6
rb,a,c,3
ra,b,c,3

analysis: dc
note: In this problem, we use the Find equivalent analysis (the calculator's er script) for the first time, on a passive circuit. Find the equivalent resistance between nodes 0 and c — the lesson walks this one through step by step.
image: https://learn.symbulator.com/assets/circuit/b11e0829.jpg [408px]
tool: er
n1: 0
n2: c
rounding: 3
si: no
units: yes


[RM3's Practice Problem 9-4]

e,1,0,3.3
r1,1,2,66
r2,2,0,24

analysis: dc
note: In this problem, we find a Thévenin / Norton equivalent (the calculator's th script) for the first time. Find the equivalent seen from nodes 2 and 0: vth, ino, req and pmax all arrive together.
image: https://learn.symbulator.com/assets/circuit/rm3pp0904.jpg [312px]
tool: th
n1: 2
n2: 0
rounding: 3
si: yes
units: yes


[B11's Example 9.6]

e,1,0,9
r1,1,2,3
r2,2,0,6

analysis: dc
note: Find the Thévenin equivalent of the shaded network, then the current through RL for values of 2, 10 and 100 ohm. The load question comes ticked, so irl appears under the four answers; the Evaluate line reads it at the 2 ohm load, 1.5 A; change the condition to load = 10 and load = 100 for the other two: 0.5 A and 0.059 A. Rounding is approx to 2 digits, as the chapter says, so the answers read as the book prints them.
image: https://learn.symbulator.com/assets/circuit/b11e0906.jpg [500px]
tool: th
n1: 2
n2: 0
with_load: yes
rounding: 2
si: no
units: yes
evaluate: irl
evaluate_conditions: load = 2


[RM3's Example 9-8]

e,1,0,24
r1,1,2,120
r2,2,0,280
j,2,0,560'm

analysis: dc
note: Find the Norton equivalent of the circuit left of a-b; the load question comes ticked, ready for the Load circuit equivalent button, which writes the next entry. Version 9 reports the Norton current in the direction it flows, so the sign differs from the calculator's 0.36.
image: https://learn.symbulator.com/assets/circuit/rm3e0908.jpg
tool: th
n1: 2
n2: 0
with_load: yes
rounding: 3
si: no
units: yes


[RM3's Example 9-8 (with the load)]

jN,0,n,iNo
rE,n,0,rEq
rL,n,0,168
j,0,n,180'm

analysis: dc
note: The equivalent from the previous entry, as the Load circuit equivalent button writes it, with the load changed to 168 ohm and the 180 mA source added. Find the current through RL: the rl block reads -0.06 A, that is, 60 mA flowing from 0 to n.
defines: iNo=-9/25
defines: rEq=84
image: https://learn.symbulator.com/assets/circuit/rm3e0908.jpg
rounding: 3
si: no
units: yes
